Turn a device's internet access on or off, on a schedule or on demand.

Adds a settings page under Firewall and a dashboard widget with per-device
toggles. Enforcement is one firewall rule against one externally managed alias
for the internet, and two rules and two such aliases for messaging, so switching
a device takes effect immediately without a ruleset reload.

Blocked devices lose internet only - local services such as printers, media
players and dashboards keep working.

A device can also be denied messaging apps (WhatsApp, Telegram, Snapchat,
Signal, LINE, Viber, KakaoTalk, and the whole Facebook, Instagram and Messenger
apps), always or outside its allowed hours, while the rest of its internet
keeps working. Blocking messaging also cuts every push notification
on the device, including iMessage and incoming FaceTime calls, for as long as
it lasts.

WWW: https://github.com/nycoagung/opnsense-plugin-parentalcontrol

Plugin Changelog
----------------

0.2.0

* Messaging blocking offers Viber, KakaoTalk, and Facebook & Instagram, which
  blocks the whole Facebook, Instagram and Messenger apps. KakaoTalk also
  blocks other Kakao services such as Daum and Kakao Map
* All eight apps are selected by default. Upgrading adds the three new ones to
  a saved selection, so a device denied messaging loses them too; a selection
  saved empty stays empty
* A protected name can belong to an app: Facebook's and Instagram's main names,
  and three of WhatsApp's, are protected only while their app is not selected
* The apps' names are looked up in turns, so a long list cannot starve a short
  one
* Viber's and KakaoTalk's networks ship with the plugin, built from ARIN's
  registry and from the prefixes Kakao announces
* The Settings tab has no Save button any more: Apply saves the settings when
  they changed, then applies them, and an invalid setting stops it
* A downloaded range that reaches into private, local, carrier-grade NAT or
  multicast address space is ignored, and the data generator refuses an app
  range that reaches into any special-purpose block
* WhatsApp's fallback chat name is blocked too: once its main chat server is
  blocked, WhatsApp moves to it within a minute or two

0.1.0

* Messaging blocking: each device has a Messaging setting (not blocked,
  blocked, or blocked outside its allowed hours) and Settings has a list of
  messaging apps to block. While a device is denied messaging all its
  notifications are cut too, including iMessage and incoming FaceTime calls
* Messaging adds two aliases (NoMessaging and Messaging_Servers) and two
  floating block rules, made the first time a device uses it. The internet
  alias can no longer be named NoMessaging or Messaging_Servers
* The Status tab shows messaging per device, with a summary line, and the
  status output gains the messaging fields
* A device address with host bits set (192.0.2.10/24) is used in its network
  form and one entered as /32 as the bare address, which is how pf keeps them
* Uninstall also removes the messaging aliases, rules and pf tables and the
  plugin's data directory, waits for a running sync, and refuses, changing
  nothing, while anything else in the configuration refers to one of the
  plugin's aliases
* An installed installer now runs the downloaded release's own installer, so a
  release that adds files installs completely. Upgrading from 0.0.2 needs the
  install run twice (see the README)

0.0.2

* Model fields are read through their typed accessors, and the scripts and
  API actions declare their types. Nothing visible changes
* The status output gives each device's enabled flag as true or false
  rather than "1" or "0"
* Hardening from static analysis: a DHCP lease line that cannot be split is
  skipped, only text addresses are taken from the pf table listing, and a
  status answer that is not a JSON object is reported as a failure

0.0.1

* Initial release
